  "summary": "The health-tech landscape today reflects several converging pressures: artificial intelligence continues reshaping clinical operations with debate over governance standards and alternative architectures like \"world models,\" while revenue cycle and interoperability challenges persist despite new platform funding and regulatory efforts. Candid Health secured $120 million for AI-driven revenue cycle management and Assured raised $19 million for provider operations, signaling investor confidence in automation of back-office functions, though questions remain about appropriate AI oversight in hospitals. On the clinical side, Merck's Ebola vaccine showed promise for broader protection in the DRC outbreak, and Science Corporation's retinal implant for blindness launched in Europe ahead of potential U.S. approval in 2027, while debate continued over whether a new cholesterol drug represents meaningful advancement. Broader workforce and access challenges also emerged: a new study found 24 percent of American workers stay in jobs primarily for health insurance coverage, and federal health quality research grants worth over $200 million faced early termination, even as proposals advanced for ACA public options and improved behavioral health prescribing practices.",
